okay so as we saw right this was a good  example uh of uh some of the work that  you will be able to do  um and uh one of the things that i want  to mention is that what you saw in this  example is just the latest part of it  right but even before that many times  we're faced with data that is not  clearly organized  not easy to deal with as an integrated  data set and each one of the data types  has  very specific properties and so the  first part that we will do in this  program is we will actually try to take  a look at some of the raw data sets and  how to process those data sets to  prepare them for analysis and turn them  into structured data  and to do that i want to briefly talk  about several different types of data  that we will cover in this particular  program so since we're called omix logic  first i will talk about the omix part of  it  so onyx data  could be generated using a variety of  technologies that are all commonly known  as high throughput automated  technologies so high throughput means  that they a single experiment produces  thousands and thousands of measurements  so a lot of measurements these  technologies are automated and so we  need to understand  what does the technology do to produce  the data that we actually study and for  that  we  will take a look at processing steps and  explain how the processing steps are  aligned with the technology itself so  here we have a few examples phenomics so  phenotypic for example imaging of cells  or tissues or organs we have genomics  typically these are variants with some  score so either the number of mutations  that we observe per position or somatic  score for example right so we need to  think about how do we actually turn that  into continuous data if we want to use  some of these models epigenomics we have  chipseek or whole genome by sulfide  sequencing where we analyze dna  methylation and modifications of  chromatin  and transcriptomic data so where we also  measure the gene levels of gene and  transcript expression but also we can  speak about alternative splicing  so now let's take a look at how do these  specific types of data  undergo  processing steps to turn them into  structured data that we can use for  these machine learning and statistical  methods so what is genomic data so  genomic and transcriptomic data a lot of  this is based on shotgun sequencing  shotgun sequencing means that we  generate short reads and then one of the  challenges is aligning those reads to  the right place on the genome so this is  a visualization of a bam file where we  can see how those reads are aligned to  different places and how can we detect  variants and so we'll spend a little bit  of time in this program to talk about  how can we analyze this kind of data in  different ways to turn it into structure  data  once this data is structured we will  then talk about what can you do with  such a table of  genomic variants  of  abundances of different regions for  histone and dna methylation  and of gene expression levels  and one of the important things here is  to understand how  different libraries in r and python can  help us prepare this data into common  structures like matrices and data frames  and the immediate application of that is  to try to understand what does this data  tell us in summary right so we can look  at individual features  how do we actually start evaluating some  of the overall properties of these data  sets and so we'll spend a little bit of  time on what's called exploratory  analysis and exploratory analysis helps  us test these assumptions what is the  distribution or what is the relationship  between individual features  after that we will turn to visualization  so one important part of exploratory  analysis is knowing how to visualize the  right kinds of data with the appropriate  methods so we'll talk about categorical  continuous  discrete data sets we'll talk about  making summaries for categorical data  using cross tabs or what's called pivot  tables we will talk about box plots and  how that helps us understand in summary  the statistical properties but also what  are some of the missing pieces of  information that we can misinterpret  just looking at box plots we'll also  talk about scatter plots and how to  produce a right representation of  continuous data we'll talk about how  this kind of a process of going from  unstructured data to structured data  helps us understand in general what's  going on but we'll also talk about the  importance of filtering ordering and  organizing our data so that we can start  finding patterns that are meaningful and  what does meaning really means  and then we will talk about in the  overall process how do these individual  steps fit into a data science approach  to these kinds of big data sets so we'll  talk about  how is data collected how then it is  prepared for exploratory analysis what  can we do to mine this data and find  important patterns and how do we deploy  strategies for data analysis statistical  modeling or machine learning to produce  reliable and reproducible  findings that we call inference right  how do we understand what the data is  telling us and how can we use that to  make next steps conclusions to ask new  questions right what comes after we  perform all of these steps and  importantly when we talk about  the kind of generalizable findings that  we have in our data how do we actually  think about the quality of our findings  so how confident can we be in our  findings how deployable and  generalizable are the models that we  train  and so there are a lot of useful  libraries and interesting examples that  we will go through in r as well as in  python and kind of really talk about  some of the  linkages between the statistical  properties of data and biological  interpretation and again because we are  going to be using curated data sets  we're also going to be paying a lot of  attention to validating what we find  both in terms of its statistical  properties but also looking at  literature and understanding why what we  are finding is a reliable pattern or a  pattern that we can test and validate  biologically so specifically i think  this is  targeting some of the people that come  from more of a biology or a clinical  background that maybe don't have as much  trust in pure statistics and machine  learning but really would like to  understand why  and so ultimately the goal for this  program is to understand how all of  these pieces are linked together and  then to be able to find the right pieces  of code that you might need or the right  tools that you might need and learn how  to use them on your own system in your  own environment  and know how to explain the process to  somebody else of how did you actually go  from where you started and where you  ended up and so briefly  to link it back to what we have been uh  doing up until now  let us uh go to the learn portal and  i'll kind of show you where you can  start with uh the program details are  going to be shared with uh by sonalika  in just a few minutes but where can you  start right so let let's say you're not  ready for the program maybe some of the  topics that we discussed today are not  really clear what can you do already  today to kind of evaluate some of these  things for yourself so if you're not  familiar with a lot of the concepts and  topics that i just shared we recommend  for you to start with a course called  bytes and molecules  this is a free course  this free course is available you know  as soon as you sign up and it's a good  way to understand some of the topics  that we briefly covered and  introduce yourself to the variety of  omics data types that we will be dealing  with throughout the program  that includes  dna variation the association between  sequence and structure understanding  next generation sequencing genomics  transcriptomics and metagenomics as well  as thinking about some of the  applications like what we saw presented  by dr  raghav how is this applied to precision  medicine how are these topics useful for  space omics and astronaut health um and  and a brief introduction to  bioinformatics analysis languages so  this is a free course  if you want to try before you commit or  you want to just explore some of these  topics i highly recommend going through  this free introduction  the other  course that i would recommend is called  introduction to bioinformatics right so  many of you might be coming from many  different backgrounds from medicine to  agriculture to biotechnology and so here  we talk a little bit about the  applications of bioinformatics in a  variety of topics that i just described  so how can this be  useful  in a variety of topics there  so the final uh thing that i want to  point to is that the full syllabus of  this program is actually available on  the programs tab right so if you go to  the programs tab you will find here  a variety of different programs  and this particular program is called  uh data science for biomedical data  analysis right uh  yeah  so uh this program is going to start on  december 14th  and we'll cover the things that we  discussed today from statistical  analysis to data visualization and  machine learning and you can find the  detailed syllabus of what topics will be  covered right here in the program and to  talk about this program syllabus and how  you can register i will pass this on  back to songarika
 

